WEBVTT

0
00:00:00.220 --> 00:00:17.920
<v Guillaume Hachez>Salut, moi, c'est Guillaume Hachez. Bienvenue dans Septante Minutes Avec. Cette semaine, un épisode qui est doublement exceptionnel.

1
00:00:18.140 --> 00:00:24.960
<v Guillaume Hachez>On m'a invité gracieusement à venir le mois dernier à la conférence SingularityU à Bruxelles, où j'ai pu enregistrer deux interviews.

2
00:00:24.980 --> 00:00:36.300
<v Guillaume Hachez>J'ai d'abord rencontré Jaya Baloo, qui est responsable de la sécurité des systèmes d'information chez KPN aux Pays-Bas. Ou plutôt, elle l'était, puisque entre-temps, elle est passée chez Avast. Elle est absolument charmante.

3
00:00:36.360 --> 00:00:43.060
<v Guillaume Hachez>On a parlé ensemble de cybersécurité, de Edward Snowden, de surveillance de masse et de l'impact de toutes ces questions sur la sphère politique.

4
00:00:43.449 --> 00:00:55.280
<v Guillaume Hachez>Et juste après ça, vous pourrez entendre mon interview avec Jamie Susskind, qui est avocat et qui a publié un livre nommé Future Politics. C'est donc un épisode qui parle technologie et c'est surtout un épisode qui est en anglais.

5
00:00:55.340 --> 00:00:58.800
<v Guillaume Hachez>Donc si vous n'êtes pas anglophone, j'ai bien peur qu'il faille attendre le prochain épisode.

6
00:00:59.640 --> 00:01:11.880
<v Guillaume Hachez>Je reviendrai dans deux petites semaines avec du pur Septante Minutes Avec en français et avec de grands invités, comme par exemple Olivia Gazalé ou bien Christophe Marchand, l'avocat belge de Julian Assange. Allez, c'est parti.

7
00:01:11.960 --> 00:01:22.980
<v Guillaume Hachez>My guest today is none other than Jaya Baloo. Jaya Baloo, you are CISO at KPN for the next week or so, because in about a week, you're about to become, you're about to switch over to Avast.

8
00:01:23.240 --> 00:01:23.500
<v Jaya Baloo + Jamie Susskind>Mm-hmm.

9
00:01:23.740 --> 00:01:24.800
<v Guillaume Hachez>Jaya Baloo, welcome to the show.

10
00:01:24.940 --> 00:01:25.380
<v Jaya Baloo + Jamie Susskind>Thank you.

11
00:01:25.640 --> 00:01:29.480
<v Guillaume Hachez>Could you perhaps introduce yourself for those that don't know you? Who are you? Where are you from?

12
00:01:29.760 --> 00:01:42.120
<v Jaya Baloo + Jamie Susskind>So my name is Jaya Baloo. I'm currently, indeed, the Chief Information Security Officer of KPN in the Netherlands. My background is born in India, grew up in the United States, because of parents who worked at the United Nations,

13
00:01:42.480 --> 00:01:45.240
<v Jaya Baloo + Jamie Susskind>and living in Europe for more than 20 years.

14
00:01:45.860 --> 00:01:48.380
<v Jaya Baloo + Jamie Susskind>So I have a quite eclectic—

15
00:01:48.430 --> 00:01:48.610
<v Guillaume Hachez>Yeah, yeah.

16
00:01:48.610 --> 00:01:48.960
<v Jaya Baloo + Jamie Susskind>... international background.

17
00:01:48.990 --> 00:01:50.800
<v Guillaume Hachez>That's very, very interesting background.

18
00:01:50.860 --> 00:01:51.100
<v Jaya Baloo + Jamie Susskind>Yeah.

19
00:01:51.380 --> 00:02:04.100
<v Guillaume Hachez>We're obviously gonna talk about cybersecurity and all that nerdy shit in just a bit. But I do wanna ask, originally from India, grew up in the United States, today working in the Netherlands, where do you feel your roots are?

20
00:02:04.340 --> 00:02:10.920
<v Jaya Baloo + Jamie Susskind>This is a really good question because I actually don't have such a problem. I believe that home is really where your heart is.

21
00:02:10.930 --> 00:02:10.960
<v Guillaume Hachez>Okay.

22
00:02:10.960 --> 00:02:19.100
<v Jaya Baloo + Jamie Susskind>And I really feel like a citizen of the world, and what I really am not such a fan of is this kind of nationalistic, regionalistic response we usually have.

23
00:02:19.480 --> 00:02:26.260
<v Jaya Baloo + Jamie Susskind>I think I identify with my species. And I would like to prefer to see myself as human—

24
00:02:26.600 --> 00:02:26.860
<v Guillaume Hachez>Okay.

25
00:02:26.880 --> 00:02:32.240
<v Jaya Baloo + Jamie Susskind>... and belonging to this planet as a bigger thing than the borders that define us.

26
00:02:32.580 --> 00:02:44.800
<v Guillaume Hachez>That's a fantastic response. I love it. As I said, we're gonna talk about crypto in just a bit, but before that, Jaya, I think you're working in cybersecurity, and I think you're a woman. What's that about?

27
00:02:44.840 --> 00:02:51.660
<v Jaya Baloo + Jamie Susskind>Yeah, I think there is still a male dominance of the industry. That's undeniable. However, I do think it's getting better.

28
00:02:51.800 --> 00:02:58.060
<v Jaya Baloo + Jamie Susskind>So you have to imagine that I have been a geek since the age of 9 or 10.

29
00:02:58.700 --> 00:03:03.560
<v Jaya Baloo + Jamie Susskind>I was the only girl that I knew of that had a Commodore 64 when it came out, and

30
00:03:04.180 --> 00:03:14.560
<v Jaya Baloo + Jamie Susskind>was programming Basic and was having fun with technology at a very early age, but it was very weird, at that time, I was the only one of my gender, and that's a bit of a shame.

31
00:03:14.620 --> 00:03:27.180
<v Jaya Baloo + Jamie Susskind>And I wasn't actually, but I just didn't always have the means to connect with other women. And then growing up and entering the field, from my early 20s, I saw that there just were not a lot of women.

32
00:03:27.220 --> 00:03:34.280
<v Jaya Baloo + Jamie Susskind>It is getting better today. There are more women, but it's still nowhere near 50%, so that's, I think, something to work on.

33
00:03:34.500 --> 00:03:45.400
<v Guillaume Hachez>So that's a recurring topic on this show. We've had many guests who discuss this topic, but in your specific case, how would you explain, like, you escaped this determinism of sorts.

34
00:03:45.500 --> 00:03:50.860
<v Guillaume Hachez>Like, there is an implicit rule that says, "Hey, nerds are men."

35
00:03:51.290 --> 00:03:51.300
<v Jaya Baloo + Jamie Susskind>Yeah.

36
00:03:51.300 --> 00:03:56.000
<v Guillaume Hachez>And you seem to have escaped that somehow. Like, how do you explain that?

37
00:03:56.280 --> 00:04:05.840
<v Jaya Baloo + Jamie Susskind>I think, like, you can't put a gender on curiosity, and you can't put a gender on a lot of qualities that we just have as people.

38
00:04:05.960 --> 00:04:19.680
<v Jaya Baloo + Jamie Susskind>I think, though, that it is not always a female-friendly place to work when you work in an industry that is so male-dominated, and you either figure out how to adapt or you rebel and do it your own way.

39
00:04:19.800 --> 00:04:26.140
<v Jaya Baloo + Jamie Susskind>I think initially I did a lot of adapting, and then I had something... And by the way, you said it was okay to curse, so then I had—

40
00:04:26.160 --> 00:04:26.210
<v Guillaume Hachez>It is.

41
00:04:26.210 --> 00:04:29.740
<v Jaya Baloo + Jamie Susskind>... something like, "Fuck it, I do it exactly how I'm gonna do it."

42
00:04:29.920 --> 00:04:30.100
<v Guillaume Hachez>Okay.

43
00:04:30.240 --> 00:04:31.360
<v Jaya Baloo + Jamie Susskind>And, you know, there is—

44
00:04:31.620 --> 00:04:34.400
<v Guillaume Hachez>Around what time would you say? How old were you when that happened?

45
00:04:35.620 --> 00:04:50.540
<v Jaya Baloo + Jamie Susskind>I think actually the independence that I had when I was really, like, earning enough money to be okay and not dependent on anyone, even though I just got married at the time.

46
00:04:50.580 --> 00:05:01.050
<v Jaya Baloo + Jamie Susskind>When I was totally independent, that's when I had this notion that nothing else matters. As long as I can take care of myself, everything else is okay, and that for me was a huge liberation.

47
00:05:01.060 --> 00:05:10.340
<v Jaya Baloo + Jamie Susskind>What I do notice, though, and I have to say this, specifically for, like, those top functions, there's less of that glass ceiling, but more of that glass floor.

48
00:05:10.380 --> 00:05:19.000
<v Jaya Baloo + Jamie Susskind>So when women get a chance to get such a job at the C level, especially in security, it's because things couldn't possibly get any worse.

49
00:05:19.540 --> 00:05:26.130
<v Jaya Baloo + Jamie Susskind>You know, it's because it's such a bad situation that like, "Oh, well, let her give it a go." And I had to—

50
00:05:26.430 --> 00:05:26.460
<v Guillaume Hachez>Oh, really?

51
00:05:26.460 --> 00:05:33.540
<v Jaya Baloo + Jamie Susskind>Yeah. So I think that that's almost more of a concern because that means that pretty much all the dials, all the counters are set against you to succeed,

52
00:05:34.300 --> 00:05:46.280
<v Jaya Baloo + Jamie Susskind>and that's a greater worry for me than this glass ceiling where you have, as a result of that glass ceiling, you have a lot of very incompetent men in a lot of these positions.

53
00:05:46.520 --> 00:05:56.590
<v Guillaume Hachez>So it seems like what you're saying is that you were able to achieve this independence, this emancipation, at least at first, mainly because of your financial situation.

54
00:05:56.600 --> 00:05:56.760
<v Jaya Baloo + Jamie Susskind>Yeah.

55
00:05:57.190 --> 00:06:15.199
<v Guillaume Hachez>And then your career was, in many ways, facilitated, because of what you describe as this glass floor, this idea that— The cybersecurity sector was a mess, and we needed strong, competent women to take care of all that.

56
00:06:15.320 --> 00:06:23.380
<v Jaya Baloo + Jamie Susskind>Yeah, I think, and, you know, it's also, like, function and organization specific, some of these things. However,

57
00:06:24.400 --> 00:06:31.900
<v Jaya Baloo + Jamie Susskind>once you've covered your Maslovian pyramid of needs, then I think you can actually stand up for something you believe in.

58
00:06:31.940 --> 00:06:33.240
<v Jaya Baloo + Jamie Susskind>But it takes a while, you know?

59
00:06:33.780 --> 00:06:33.920
<v Guillaume Hachez>Right.

60
00:06:34.020 --> 00:06:35.320
<v Jaya Baloo + Jamie Susskind>It's not a natural—

61
00:06:35.420 --> 00:06:35.770
<v Guillaume Hachez>Of course.

62
00:06:35.770 --> 00:06:38.660
<v Jaya Baloo + Jamie Susskind>... innate response. And if it is, then you're one of the few.

63
00:06:38.770 --> 00:06:47.929
<v Guillaume Hachez>Correct me if I'm wrong, but I feel like in the past eight years or so, cybersecurity has started to make headlines more so than it did before.

64
00:06:48.040 --> 00:06:51.880
<v Guillaume Hachez>A big part of that seems to me had to do with the Snowden revelations.

65
00:06:51.940 --> 00:06:52.160
<v Jaya Baloo + Jamie Susskind>Mm-hmm.

66
00:06:52.440 --> 00:06:56.800
<v Guillaume Hachez>So we had a mix of sorts between cybersecurity and politics.

67
00:06:57.040 --> 00:06:57.220
<v Jaya Baloo + Jamie Susskind>Mm-hmm.

68
00:06:57.300 --> 00:07:02.740
<v Guillaume Hachez>Can you talk perhaps about what's changed for your field in the past decade?

69
00:07:02.860 --> 00:07:04.340
<v Jaya Baloo + Jamie Susskind>Yeah, so Snowden definitely helped,

70
00:07:04.640 --> 00:07:18.240
<v Jaya Baloo + Jamie Susskind>but I think the issue is, like every technology, when you have a certain amount of democratization and you lower the inertia in order to actually launch an attack, so when the actual tools of the trade become much more widely spread,

71
00:07:18.680 --> 00:07:28.060
<v Jaya Baloo + Jamie Susskind>when it's clear that we have governments who are engaging in a lot of the things that they were always engaging with, but now in a sort of new area of cyber,

72
00:07:28.640 --> 00:07:31.480
<v Jaya Baloo + Jamie Susskind>and we wake up to that, that's what changed a decade ago.

73
00:07:32.240 --> 00:07:43.880
<v Jaya Baloo + Jamie Susskind>A lot of people, a lot of technical folks, they all kind of suspected what was happening, but without Snowden's revelations coming out with The Guardian, before that, we didn't really know, and I think that's the difference.

74
00:07:44.260 --> 00:07:44.270
<v Guillaume Hachez>Mm-hmm.

75
00:07:44.280 --> 00:07:53.300
<v Jaya Baloo + Jamie Susskind>So suspecting with, okay, we think they have this capability. And by the way, I worked in lawful interception. I knew exactly what things were possible from a technical perspective.

76
00:07:53.310 --> 00:07:53.320
<v Guillaume Hachez>Right.

77
00:07:53.320 --> 00:07:53.400
<v Jaya Baloo + Jamie Susskind>But—

78
00:07:53.410 --> 00:07:56.580
<v Guillaume Hachez>But before Snowden, it was kind of a tinfoil hat kind of thing, right?

79
00:07:56.680 --> 00:08:01.700
<v Jaya Baloo + Jamie Susskind>Yeah, it was. Absolutely a tinfoil hat thing. And you definitely think like, "Oh no, governments are good. They wouldn't do that."

80
00:08:01.880 --> 00:08:02.020
<v Guillaume Hachez>Right.

81
00:08:02.160 --> 00:08:07.760
<v Jaya Baloo + Jamie Susskind>Or, "Governments cooperate with telcos. They're our friends," you know? And then you saw what happened at Belgacom.

82
00:08:07.980 --> 00:08:08.060
<v Guillaume Hachez>Right.

83
00:08:08.090 --> 00:08:14.400
<v Jaya Baloo + Jamie Susskind>And then you realized, okay, even the people who we thought are our friends are in this space, obviously not so friendly.

84
00:08:14.540 --> 00:08:22.340
<v Jaya Baloo + Jamie Susskind>And that cooperation between GCHQ and NSA, that was when I had just entered KPN, and that was a little bit mind-boggling.

85
00:08:22.440 --> 00:08:31.340
<v Jaya Baloo + Jamie Susskind>So I think we've learned to expect it as the norm rather than see it as the one anomaly.

86
00:08:31.540 --> 00:08:41.560
<v Guillaume Hachez>Right. And so this change of mindset, this change of perspective on cybersecurity, did it have, like, concrete effects?

87
00:08:41.700 --> 00:08:45.720
<v Guillaume Hachez>Did it change the way you work and the way this industry operates?

88
00:08:45.980 --> 00:08:55.380
<v Jaya Baloo + Jamie Susskind>Frankly, I think there's been too little impact felt by the revelations. And the reason I say that is because that impact isn't universal.

89
00:08:55.420 --> 00:09:07.780
<v Jaya Baloo + Jamie Susskind>If we are only convincing the tinfoil hatters, that small population that already took precautions to protect their privacy and their data, then that's not the population that I would say is really needed to make that impact.

90
00:09:08.220 --> 00:09:15.789
<v Jaya Baloo + Jamie Susskind>You want legislation to change. And take a look at the legislation in all of these countries where this stuff, these revelations came out.

91
00:09:16.220 --> 00:09:30.259
<v Jaya Baloo + Jamie Susskind>You know, whether it's the United States or the UK, even though there was a UK court saying that the cooperation between the NSA and GCHQ was unlawful, there wasn't really significant change in the legislation to prevent it from happening again.

92
00:09:30.900 --> 00:09:47.000
<v Jaya Baloo + Jamie Susskind>And we haven't really learned. If anything, we've gotten more hungry and avaricious for that information dominance than more towards trying to protect information assurance of our own states.

93
00:09:47.140 --> 00:09:49.080
<v Jaya Baloo + Jamie Susskind>So we're getting hungrier for signals intelligence.

94
00:09:49.380 --> 00:09:58.849
<v Guillaume Hachez>Do you have an opinion on that? Like, how would you explain this passivity, this lack of change in the legislation area?

95
00:09:59.270 --> 00:10:05.960
<v Guillaume Hachez>'Cause we've certainly seen European members of parliaments being shocked and denouncing the actions of the NSA.

96
00:10:06.200 --> 00:10:06.210
<v Jaya Baloo + Jamie Susskind>But I think, yeah.

97
00:10:06.240 --> 00:10:09.760
<v Guillaume Hachez>But it certainly seems like there is no repercussion to that.

98
00:10:09.800 --> 00:10:09.960
<v Jaya Baloo + Jamie Susskind>Well,

99
00:10:10.400 --> 00:10:18.770
<v Jaya Baloo + Jamie Susskind>I think this has to be weighed with the current climate of the different geopolitical threats we're seeing. So if we see other state actors employing the same tactics,

100
00:10:19.200 --> 00:10:31.040
<v Jaya Baloo + Jamie Susskind>but we feel that our own intelligence agencies or our own law enforcement agencies are somehow crippled because of lack of powers they have for their own investigative things, then we don't wanna have that.

101
00:10:31.080 --> 00:10:39.180
<v Jaya Baloo + Jamie Susskind>So we wanna correct that imbalance, which is a natural need to correct that imbalance of capability of yourself versus these other potentially hostile countries.

102
00:10:39.610 --> 00:10:49.540
<v Jaya Baloo + Jamie Susskind>And the rise of different terrorist incidents give a lot of public support for these privacy-limiting actions.

103
00:10:50.280 --> 00:10:59.970
<v Jaya Baloo + Jamie Susskind>And you know, there's a great quote, now I'm gonna forget, but someone who's willing to give up their privacy for security is deserving of neither.

104
00:11:00.560 --> 00:11:02.279
<v Guillaume Hachez>Right. Yeah, yeah. I've heard that as well. Yeah.

105
00:11:02.290 --> 00:11:07.500
<v Jaya Baloo + Jamie Susskind>Yeah, I forget who that quote is, but I mean, that's really the case that should be applied here. We just don't apply it with any form of rigor.

106
00:11:07.780 --> 00:11:16.830
<v Guillaume Hachez>Part of that I think is the idea that the general public doesn't really have clear visibility on what's happening. Or

107
00:11:17.580 --> 00:11:24.120
<v Guillaume Hachez>it has access to this information, but it doesn't necessarily have clarity, it doesn't necessarily understand because it's a very complex topic.

108
00:11:24.260 --> 00:11:32.060
<v Jaya Baloo + Jamie Susskind>Well, I think the problem really is also that people still feel, and I'm amazed by that, that they still, like, kind of sit in the camp of, "I have nothing to hide."

109
00:11:32.300 --> 00:11:32.520
<v Guillaume Hachez>Right.

110
00:11:32.940 --> 00:11:35.480
<v Jaya Baloo + Jamie Susskind>Or, you know, "They're not after me."

111
00:11:36.200 --> 00:11:36.380
<v Guillaume Hachez>Right.

112
00:11:36.420 --> 00:11:38.320
<v Jaya Baloo + Jamie Susskind>"That's fine." You know? It's just—

113
00:11:38.450 --> 00:11:40.120
<v Guillaume Hachez>Or, "It's worth it because of the terrorists."

114
00:11:40.180 --> 00:11:51.540
<v Jaya Baloo + Jamie Susskind>Yeah, yeah. And to be honest, like, they're probably not after you, but that you have nothing to hide is not true, and that you are taken in the maelstrom of all of the collection that's happening, that's really the concern.

115
00:11:51.560 --> 00:11:54.930
<v Jaya Baloo + Jamie Susskind>And is that acceptable? Is that the type of country or state that we want to live in?

116
00:11:55.100 --> 00:11:55.240
<v Guillaume Hachez>Right.

117
00:11:55.260 --> 00:12:03.470
<v Jaya Baloo + Jamie Susskind>I think those are, like, more fundamental questions to ask, and do citizens care enough to have those answered or want to know how that really works?

118
00:12:03.700 --> 00:12:05.370
<v Jaya Baloo + Jamie Susskind>That is sometimes the dilemma that we're really in.

119
00:12:05.600 --> 00:12:13.500
<v Guillaume Hachez>There is a clear difference between direct targeted surveillance and mass surveillance, which is what Snowden has revealed to the world.

120
00:12:14.236 --> 00:12:20.776
<v Guillaume Hachez>How would you qualify, how would you describe the specific dangers of mass surveillance?

121
00:12:20.996 --> 00:12:31.116
<v Jaya Baloo + Jamie Susskind>Well, the real thing that you have to worry about... Look, and for most countries in Europe, we live in a democratic state, and there's a lot of emphasis on civil liberties, thank goodness.

122
00:12:31.356 --> 00:12:43.476
<v Jaya Baloo + Jamie Susskind>But I think the issue that you have to be afraid of is that in places where this type of power goes unchecked, it leads to the use of this type of data for political purposes,

123
00:12:44.156 --> 00:12:53.146
<v Jaya Baloo + Jamie Susskind>to suppress free speech, to prevent free trials, to follow journalists, to do all kinds of evil things that we would associate with a dictatorship.

124
00:12:53.976 --> 00:12:57.836
<v Jaya Baloo + Jamie Susskind>And one could argue, "No, you can trust us with it because we're good."

125
00:12:58.326 --> 00:12:58.336
<v Guillaume Hachez>Right.

126
00:12:58.336 --> 00:13:05.776
<v Jaya Baloo + Jamie Susskind>But the... without a checks and balance, without actually being able to know it, and that's why, you know, then there isn't mass surveillance, there's targeted lawful interception.

127
00:13:06.116 --> 00:13:19.146
<v Jaya Baloo + Jamie Susskind>That's why we want those kinds of privacy preserving principles where every use of power is given by a valid authority. And that mass collection could lead to more problems than that it actually solves.

128
00:13:19.146 --> 00:13:23.416
<v Guillaume Hachez>Have we seen, have we witnessed cases of this power being abused?

129
00:13:23.616 --> 00:13:34.056
<v Jaya Baloo + Jamie Susskind>Yeah, I think we have examples of it. And whether it's being used, abused by the state or whether it's being abused by individuals, we've seen examples of when the capability is available,

130
00:13:34.656 --> 00:13:37.956
<v Jaya Baloo + Jamie Susskind>it will be used and not always by the correct actor that it was intended for.

131
00:13:38.356 --> 00:13:40.456
<v Jaya Baloo + Jamie Susskind>That we've seen across different geographies in Europe.

132
00:13:40.996 --> 00:13:54.916
<v Jaya Baloo + Jamie Susskind>You know, it can go far back as, I think it was 2000, 2009 or 2012, but we saw the issue in Greece with the lawful intercept equipment that was kept there, where the auditing trails were turned off and it was being used by a host of

133
00:13:55.336 --> 00:13:58.636
<v Jaya Baloo + Jamie Susskind>other actors than the legitimate ones, to follow all types of people.

134
00:13:58.936 --> 00:14:13.116
<v Jaya Baloo + Jamie Susskind>We see those powers being abused, sometimes to follow particular, like, political parties, or journalists. There was an investigation actually, I think we need coffee, but—

135
00:14:15.136 --> 00:14:19.556
<v Jaya Baloo + Jamie Susskind>No, but we know about all types of things that are not in Europe, right?

136
00:14:19.796 --> 00:14:31.846
<v Jaya Baloo + Jamie Susskind>So we know about Egypt, which did this. We know about, there's a whole documentation on people who bought, how do you say it, entire malware kits—

137
00:14:31.976 --> 00:14:32.056
<v Guillaume Hachez>Right.

138
00:14:32.066 --> 00:14:36.696
<v Jaya Baloo + Jamie Susskind>... in order to look at what their population was doing. So we know about that type of abuse.

139
00:14:37.076 --> 00:14:46.216
<v Jaya Baloo + Jamie Susskind>But in Europe, whether we actually know, the only example that I have, like, directly on the top of my mind is Greece, but I know that there were incidences of following journalists, et cetera—

140
00:14:46.286 --> 00:14:46.296
<v Guillaume Hachez>Right.

141
00:14:46.296 --> 00:14:58.676
<v Jaya Baloo + Jamie Susskind>... in other geographies in Europe. There was a report brought out by the clients of hacking software that were used to get, like, information from very specific targeted groups.

142
00:14:59.176 --> 00:15:01.336
<v Jaya Baloo + Jamie Susskind>And then, and again, that was being used all over the planet.

143
00:15:01.456 --> 00:15:13.416
