438
00:45:14.356 --> 00:45:27.156
<v Guillaume Hachez>then you shouldn't use it. Might be a machine learning system, might be another system. But for instance, there are courts in the United States where if you're being sentenced, they will use a machine learning system to read data about you which

439
00:45:27.976 --> 00:45:31.076
<v Guillaume Hachez>purports to predict how likely you are to reoffend.

440
00:45:31.956 --> 00:45:39.696
<v Guillaume Hachez>And the judge, it doesn't determine your sentence, but the judge is entitled to take that into account when he sentences you or when she sentences you.

441
00:45:40.436 --> 00:45:47.776
<v Guillaume Hachez>And there's been quite a lot of legal upset about that in the United States, particularly because the Supreme Court of Wisconsin

442
00:45:48.916 --> 00:45:51.536
<v Guillaume Hachez>argued that it was lawful to use such systems

443
00:45:51.996 --> 00:46:04.396
<v Guillaume Hachez>even when the person being sentenced wasn't able to access the data and algorithms that had been used in part to calculate the length of their sentence, and the American Supreme Court declined to hear the case.

444
00:46:04.436 --> 00:46:13.426
<v Guillaume Hachez>So yes, this stuff is real. Right. I mean, algorithms are also used for things like predictive policing, or to determine whether certain prisoners get bail.

445
00:46:14.136 --> 00:46:22.836
<v Guillaume Hachez>I can't think of any society in which they are currently the last word, so they're not decisive, although they might be in an individual case.

446
00:46:23.276 --> 00:46:35.106
<v Guillaume Hachez>It certainly sounds very deterministic. It sounds like, based on where you're from, what your name is, what your gender is, these are things that might influence the algorithm and,

447
00:46:35.816 --> 00:46:39.256
<v Guillaume Hachez>have an important impact on, for instance, your jail sentence.

448
00:46:39.316 --> 00:46:48.176
<v Guillaume Hachez>Exactly. Jamie, are you a pessimist? Should we be scared of the future? Is this the message of your book that we should be scared of what the future has in hold for us?

449
00:46:48.396 --> 00:46:59.076
<v Guillaume Hachez>Not scared, no, and no, I'm not a pessimist, but I can't describe myself as an optimist either. What I say is that it's possible now to see coming over the horizon these big problems, these big challenges.

450
00:46:59.856 --> 00:47:12.456
<v Guillaume Hachez>What are we gonna do about them? Are we going to let the technologies of tomorrow develop according simply to market logic, getting on with it themselves, or are we going to step in and regulate and legislate and

451
00:47:13.076 --> 00:47:18.156
<v Guillaume Hachez>bring them under the control of a wider population for the good of the public?

452
00:47:18.656 --> 00:47:25.616
<v Guillaume Hachez>If we take that choice, the latter choice, then we'll be in a good position because we'll be able to harness all of the awesome and amazing things that technology can bring us,

453
00:47:26.296 --> 00:47:32.116
<v Guillaume Hachez>while at the same time preserving and augmenting our democracy and our freedom and our justice.

454
00:47:32.536 --> 00:47:46.956
<v Guillaume Hachez>But if we simply sit back and let things take their natural course, then there's every reason to be pessimistic because there's no reason why technologies developed according to commercial logic should also be good for society. Mm.

455
00:47:47.086 --> 00:47:50.716
<v Guillaume Hachez>... and for politics. There's no reason at all. Part of the challenge, I think, is that

456
00:47:51.416 --> 00:48:06.256
<v Guillaume Hachez>most of the innovation we are witnessing in the tech industry comes from Silicon Valley, comes from the United States, which is a place that cherishes this idea of entrepreneurship, of risk-taking, of innovation, and where there is

457
00:48:06.956 --> 00:48:15.216
<v Guillaume Hachez>relatively less legislation, less of a tight grip on those companies by the states.

458
00:48:15.756 --> 00:48:22.276
<v Guillaume Hachez>From what you're saying, it sounds like you're a partisan of more legislation. Do you believe this is something that will happen?

459
00:48:22.656 --> 00:48:35.206
<v Guillaume Hachez>I think your diagnosis is absolutely correct. I think that a lot of the tech that comes out of America comes out of America because of the sort of Wild West nature of the entrepreneurial environment there.

460
00:48:35.726 --> 00:48:44.886
<v Guillaume Hachez>There's also things like the First Amendment to the US Constitution, which makes it very difficult for states to intervene in the free market where it's considered that it might have implications for free speech.

461
00:48:45.552 --> 00:48:52.592
<v Guillaume Hachez>And yes, the Americans would look towards us here in Europe and they would say, "Have you ever wondered why you don't have a Twitter or a Facebook or a Google?"

462
00:48:52.892 --> 00:49:07.412
<v Guillaume Hachez>And there's probably some merit in that question. They're getting at something there. I believe that it is up to Europe, as I think it is already doing, to propose and to show through success an alternative model.

463
00:49:07.872 --> 00:49:22.012
<v Guillaume Hachez>I don't think the American way is the only way of doing it. If we want to chuck all our laws down the toilet in pursuit of naked entrepreneurship and the maximization of unicorns and billion-dollar, trillion-dollar companies, then we can,

464
00:49:22.792 --> 00:49:24.212
<v Guillaume Hachez>but that doesn't mean we should.

465
00:49:24.752 --> 00:49:33.022
<v Guillaume Hachez>And so I think what you'll see, and what I would encourage, is the emergence of basically three global models, the American model, the European model, and the Chinese model.

466
00:49:34.732 --> 00:49:42.052
<v Guillaume Hachez>And I am not shy about arguing for a European model, to answer your question, which involves some regulation, some legislation,

467
00:49:43.212 --> 00:49:55.412
<v Guillaume Hachez>which on the one hand seeks to preserve an innovative culture and free speech and the like, but on the other hand recognizes that technologies have consequences and that some things are more important than making a quick buck.

468
00:49:55.912 --> 00:50:04.032
<v Guillaume Hachez>That is a very crude way of putting it, but it's the essence of my argument. Right. In fact, in my next book, if I may— Go ahead ...

469
00:50:04.312 --> 00:50:15.492
<v Guillaume Hachez>I'll be making the case for regulation and saying, "Here's why we should have regulation. Here's what a regulator should look like, and here's what the regulations themselves should look like."

470
00:50:15.532 --> 00:50:25.332
<v Guillaume Hachez>And I'm sure it will be greeted with hostility in certain parts of the States. So Europe is certainly a challenge, but to go back for a second to Silicon Valley, how do we even

471
00:50:26.152 --> 00:50:39.692
<v Guillaume Hachez>get those tech giants to put themselves in question and to see the danger they can bring to our democracies and our societies, such as in the case of Cambridge Analytica?

472
00:50:39.832 --> 00:50:46.852
<v Guillaume Hachez>Do we have any hope that such a thing could happen? Putting it simply, sometimes tech firms will just do the right thing.

473
00:50:46.912 --> 00:50:54.071
<v Guillaume Hachez>You know, Microsoft claims to follow the GDPR rules even though it's not strictly subject to them in some respects. Good for Microsoft.

474
00:50:55.432 --> 00:51:04.092
<v Guillaume Hachez>Sometimes public outrage will force companies or at least encourage them to take a different path. I think that is the case with Facebook over the last few years.

475
00:51:04.132 --> 00:51:13.372
<v Guillaume Hachez>I'm not saying it's been doing a good job, but it's certainly been taking more public efforts to regulate itself. But I'm very clear about this.

476
00:51:13.632 --> 00:51:21.152
<v Guillaume Hachez>You can't rely on the benevolence or the wisdom of— Right ... tech firms. And I do think it's a bit of a dodge sometimes.

477
00:51:21.212 --> 00:51:35.032
<v Guillaume Hachez>I think we— Well, as you said, it's kind of a Wild West nature over there, so. Right. But we underestimate sometimes the consequences that national or things like supranational in the form of the EU regulation can have.

478
00:51:35.832 --> 00:51:43.562
<v Guillaume Hachez>We simply are better protected here in Europe in terms of our data usage because of the GDPR than our brothers and sisters in America are.

479
00:51:44.512 --> 00:51:54.252
<v Guillaume Hachez>We are better protected, and I'm sure the companies in question find it more onerous to operate in Europe as a consequence, but so be it.

480
00:51:54.312 --> 00:52:05.052
<v Guillaume Hachez>They still do. It's a huge market. The fact that, for instance, it is very difficult to tax big multinational companies doesn't mean that we don't pass tax law and try to tax them.

481
00:52:05.592 --> 00:52:18.532
<v Guillaume Hachez>It just means that it's not always 100% successful. Now, I recognize the difficulties with regulating tech firms. The biggest risk is obviously always that you make one area so uncompetitive

482
00:52:19.392 --> 00:52:28.402
<v Guillaume Hachez>that it doesn't enjoy the flow of business that other areas might have, which is why continental-wide legislation is preferable to national legislation.

483
00:52:29.472 --> 00:52:40.792
<v Guillaume Hachez>But at the same time, you know, I think the German government said to Twitter, "It's unlawful to show a swastika in this country, and if you show a swastika, if someone tweets a swastika, we're gonna hold you responsible."

484
00:52:41.372 --> 00:52:46.512
<v Guillaume Hachez>Lo and behold, if I tweet a swastika in England and someone looks at it in Germany, they won't see it.

485
00:52:47.892 --> 00:52:58.592
<v Guillaume Hachez>If companies were given incentives to find national solutions to their regulatory problems more often, I suspect, with their great genius, they would find ways of doing so.

486
00:52:58.832 --> 00:53:13.692
<v Guillaume Hachez>You're saying that the changes we make here in Europe, here in the European Union, however difficult it might be, will at some point have a global effect on what's happening for our brothers and sisters in the US.

487
00:53:13.972 --> 00:53:18.292
<v Guillaume Hachez>Maybe, although that isn't their primary aim. Their primary aim is to— Right ... is to look after people in Europe. Yeah.

488
00:53:18.302 --> 00:53:23.592
<v Guillaume Hachez>And sometimes they do. Part of the challenge here is that, you know, we've seen,

489
00:53:24.432 --> 00:53:34.772
<v Guillaume Hachez>I think it was like about a year ago, Mark Zuckerberg in front of Congress, and he was being asked, "How come your company is able to make money if you're not charging your customers?"

490
00:53:35.272 --> 00:53:47.312
<v Guillaume Hachez>Obviously, that was an edge case. I'm hoping that our politicians in Europe are a bit more tech-savvy than that. However, that's definitely part of the problem, isn't it?

491
00:53:47.592 --> 00:54:04.632
<v Guillaume Hachez>Again, we're going back to the complexity of these issues, and we're having these lawmakers, which are often kind of the, you know, senior types, and we're asking them to legislate on stuff that's very hard to grasp.

492
00:54:04.692 --> 00:54:07.832
<v Guillaume Hachez>So isn't that part of the issue, again, the complexity of these,

493
00:54:08.092 --> 00:54:18.112
<v Guillaume Hachez>of these issues? Yeah. If you're asking me do I have a great deal of confidence in the current generation of lawmakers in the Western world to produce the wise rules and laws that are needed,

494
00:54:18.872 --> 00:54:20.172
<v Guillaume Hachez>the answer is no, I don't.

495
00:54:20.612 --> 00:54:28.212
<v Guillaume Hachez>It's partly generational for the reasons you described. There are still— I still meet politicians who will proudly tell me that they don't know how to use email, proudly.

496
00:54:28.642 --> 00:54:37.592
<v Guillaume Hachez>And I just sort of think, "Well, if that's what you take pride in, you're unlikely to be able to say much of use about machine learning algorithms."

497
00:54:38.452 --> 00:54:48.772
<v Guillaume Hachez>So it's partly a generational thing. It's partly a political will thing. Politicians now are a lot more educated on climate change than they were 15 years ago.

498
00:54:49.332 --> 00:54:56.752
<v Guillaume Hachez>The reason for that is 'cause it's risen up the political agenda. It's no longer acceptable not to understand the science, whether you agree or disagree with it.

499
00:54:57.584 --> 00:55:06.714
<v Guillaume Hachez>And I would like to see something similar happening with technology. It's why I do what I do. I don't think you need to be a computer scientist or a software— Mm-hmm ...

500
00:55:06.714 --> 00:55:14.304
<v Guillaume Hachez>engineer to understand the social implications of these technologies, but you do need to have a passing understanding of them, which comes from some kind of curiosity.

501
00:55:15.064 --> 00:55:23.024
<v Guillaume Hachez>And you need to have a passing understanding of the literature that's been written about them. And there actually are quite a lot of smart policy types,

502
00:55:24.024 --> 00:55:28.824
<v Guillaume Hachez>not politicians themselves, but people working for politicians, who are increasingly interested in this stuff.

503
00:55:29.284 --> 00:55:36.264
<v Guillaume Hachez>I have been encouraged by that, but we're nowhere near where we need to be. You are making a comparison with climate change.

504
00:55:36.984 --> 00:55:48.324
<v Guillaume Hachez>There is a definite urgency with climate issues. I don't think you can say, or can you actually say, that there is a sense of urgency when talking about these tech issues?

505
00:55:48.664 --> 00:55:57.884
<v Guillaume Hachez>Well, it's funny you say that. I mean, I think our generation sees there as being urgency with climate change. Right. I think a lot of people don't see it that way.

506
00:55:58.044 --> 00:56:09.524
<v Guillaume Hachez>I think they think, "So the sea's going to rise a bit at an indeterminate point in the future because of a vast aggregation of different sources of carbon emission across the planet."

507
00:56:10.164 --> 00:56:17.864
<v Guillaume Hachez>In a sense, one of the difficulties with climate change is that it lacks an urgency. You can't say, "This time next year, San Francisco will be underwater."

508
00:56:18.724 --> 00:56:34.624
<v Guillaume Hachez>And to that extent, I think it shares an issue with technology. What you're describing is an incremental process of change with tech, albeit one that I think is accelerating in speed rather than decelerating, and there's no...

509
00:56:34.684 --> 00:56:45.494
<v Guillaume Hachez>There are seldom flashpoints where things come to a head. That said, the reason that Zuckerberg was before Congress is because of a sense that there might have been Russian interference in American elections

510
00:56:45.964 --> 00:56:52.604
<v Guillaume Hachez>or that Cambridge Analytica had had an unwanted effect on the outcome of those elections.

511
00:56:52.904 --> 00:57:00.564
<v Guillaume Hachez>Likewise, there have been similar rumblings in England after the Brexit referendum, and I make no comment about that. But

512
00:57:01.664 --> 00:57:17.164
<v Guillaume Hachez>what I predict is you will have moments of crisis punctuated by long periods of nothingness, and what I try to do in my work is try to raise technology, albeit that it is something that is difficult to grasp, as an urgent policy problem,

513
00:57:17.944 --> 00:57:20.064
<v Guillaume Hachez>but I want to raise it to the top of the political agenda.

514
00:57:20.204 --> 00:57:28.304
<v Guillaume Hachez>Because if we wait till it's too late, then we'll be in big trouble. One topic we have not been talking about thus far is the idea of privacy.

515
00:57:28.684 --> 00:57:42.864
<v Guillaume Hachez>Six years ago, I think it was, Edward Snowden came forward with his infamous revelations. I don't know about you, but I don't get the feeling that a lot has changed, again, with the common sense of urgency.

516
00:57:43.184 --> 00:57:56.984
<v Guillaume Hachez>I don't see a lot of people around me worrying about their privacy being violated. How do you explain that? It's hard to know what's happening inside the US government and whether there is a more strict approach taken to acts of

517
00:57:58.344 --> 00:58:01.524
<v Guillaume Hachez>questionable legality than were in the past.

518
00:58:02.104 --> 00:58:08.804
<v Guillaume Hachez>So I can't comment on that. I was more talking about the broad population. Yeah. Yeah. I mean, it is an interesting thing that

519
00:58:10.264 --> 00:58:24.004
<v Guillaume Hachez>privacy, which I actually think is a bit of a '90s word, I don't find it that helpful a term, but it has never become the dominant political concern that perhaps some people thought it was.

520
00:58:24.044 --> 00:58:35.264
<v Guillaume Hachez>Some people might have seen the Snowden revelations and been glad that that kind of surveillance was taking place and felt safer because of it and not cared because most of it was done by machines rather than by human beings.

521
00:58:35.904 --> 00:58:43.104
<v Guillaume Hachez>That is a respectable point of view, albeit that many people would disagree with it and say that it should be regulated by law in any event.

522
00:58:43.964 --> 00:58:57.794
<v Guillaume Hachez>But what's funny is that we have these outbursts of concern about what the state does. The real threat to privacy is from private entities, not the state— Right ...

523
00:58:57.804 --> 00:59:04.764
<v Guillaume Hachez>which gather data about us and, and I think a lot of people would be staggered— Right. But— ... if they were showed what private companies knew about them.

524
00:59:04.964 --> 00:59:13.184
<v Guillaume Hachez>But I will say that part of the Snowden revelations was that the NSA had access to the data being collected by Google, by Microsoft, and so on.

525
00:59:13.704 --> 00:59:26.524
<v Guillaume Hachez>So you know, it's actually both. Absolutely. And how the state gets its hands on that data is always going to be an interesting question.

526
00:59:26.924 --> 00:59:37.804
<v Guillaume Hachez>Does it get its hands on it commercially by buying it? Does it get its hands on it legally by requiring it? Does it get its hands on it illegally by hacking into it?

527
00:59:37.814 --> 00:59:45.484
<v Guillaume Hachez>These are all questions which a free society should debate about what the appropriate role of the state is in mass surveillance.

528
00:59:46.124 --> 00:59:52.204
<v Guillaume Hachez>I do think if most people had a sense about what was known about them, they would be surprised, though. Right.

529
00:59:52.784 --> 01:00:00.164
<v Guillaume Hachez>So again, part of the issue is this complexity, the fact that not everyone understands what's going on. Yeah. Although, how complex is it?

530
01:00:00.424 --> 01:00:08.504
<v Guillaume Hachez>How complex is it to teach someone that every time they use an app, it gathers data about their location, or every time they take a photo of themselves for a particular app,

531
01:00:09.034 --> 01:00:19.444
<v Guillaume Hachez>that photo is stored in a database and used to train artificial intelligence systems or might be sold to the government to use to develop face recognition technology to survey the public?

532
01:00:20.284 --> 01:00:26.444
<v Guillaume Hachez>I don't think these concepts would be on the grasp of ordinary people. You don't need to understand how the technology works.

533
01:00:26.504 --> 01:00:35.544
<v Guillaume Hachez>Mm-hmm. But I do think it's all explainable. If it's explainable, how then can we explain the fact that people seem not to care?

534
01:00:35.744 --> 01:00:43.184
<v Guillaume Hachez>Well, maybe some people know about it but don't care. Maybe the fact that it's explainable doesn't necessarily mean that it's been explained.

535
01:00:43.244 --> 01:00:53.424
<v Guillaume Hachez>Right. And you know, I have realistic expectations about what one can do. For instance, in my line of work, I write a book.

536
01:00:54.044 --> 01:01:02.984
<v Guillaume Hachez>Some people will read it, some people won't read it. The contents of it will be reported, but many of its contents won't be reported.

537
01:01:03.344 --> 01:01:06.664
<v Guillaume Hachez>Public education about a topic as big as this is a difficult thing.

538
01:01:07.834 --> 01:01:18.094
<v Guillaume Hachez>It's particularly difficult in circumstances where a lot of very powerful entities, be them states or corporations, don't really have an interest in public education of this kind, even without being conspiratorial.

539
01:01:18.594 --> 01:01:27.054
<v Guillaume Hachez>There's just no great social demand for it or commercial demand for it from their perspective. People are just trying to get on with their lives most of the time.

540
01:01:27.254 --> 01:01:32.474
<v Guillaume Hachez>Yeah. The same, you can ask about any political question, why aren't more people angry about this? The answer is because they're just trying to get their kids to school on time.

541
01:01:32.554 --> 01:01:49.434
<v Guillaume Hachez>Right. Again, there is very little urgency in this case. Going back to this idea of an increasingly quantified society of data being collected about everyone all the time, could we take a second and explain to our listeners

542
01:01:50.314 --> 01:01:56.484
<v Guillaume Hachez>how dangerous that is and why it's problematic that data is being collected about us all the time, if that is your opinion, of course?

543
01:01:56.884 --> 01:02:07.894
<v Guillaume Hachez>Right. So we generate about as much data every couple of hours now as we did from the dawn of time until 2003, and what it means is that us as individuals, we as individuals leave behind us a

544
01:02:08.974 --> 01:02:20.754
<v Guillaume Hachez>data trail of what we buy, where we go, who we hang out with, who we speak to, what we say, what we care about, which paints a very detailed picture of our inner and outer lives,

545
01:02:21.614 --> 01:02:26.594
<v Guillaume Hachez>which politicians and kings and priests of the past would have been envious of.

546
01:02:27.014 --> 01:02:37.274
<v Guillaume Hachez>We are revealed to a much greater extent. Like, to a certain extent, it's not a bad thing. A lot of the comfort and creativity that we draw from technology, the useful services that it will provide to us in the future,

547
01:02:37.574 --> 01:02:40.614
<v Guillaume Hachez>will be precisely because so much data is being gathered and

548
01:02:41.134 --> 01:02:44.234
<v Guillaume Hachez>and we are truly known as people, truly understood.

549
01:02:44.894 --> 01:02:56.184
<v Guillaume Hachez>But at the same time, the more you know about someone, the easier it is to manipulate them and to influence them, and the person doing the manipulating or the influencing might be a commercial entity or it might be a political entity.

550
01:02:56.194 --> 01:03:08.154
<v Guillaume Hachez>But either way, we are more naked to that kind of power and more defenseless to it than we were in the past. It's probably not a huge problem if we're at least aware of it and can be vigilant against it.

551
01:03:08.234 --> 01:03:10.934
<v Guillaume Hachez>But for the reasons we were discussing a moment ago, I think a lot of us aren't,

552
01:03:11.594 --> 01:03:24.554
<v Guillaume Hachez>and we are therefore endowing certain groups in society, those who have access to that data about us, with enormous power for the future, which they may well use for good ends, but

553
01:03:25.914 --> 01:03:27.754
<v Guillaume Hachez>should we be relying on trust?

554
01:03:27.834 --> 01:03:36.894
<v Guillaume Hachez>It seems like this power, which used to be held by the states, by governments, is often now being held by these tech giants.

555
01:03:37.934 --> 01:03:49.694
<v Guillaume Hachez>Currency is one example with cryptocurrencies, information, the way we are being informed. A couple of decades ago, it used to be the public broadcaster, you know, the BBC was the main way we were all being informed.

556
01:03:50.114 --> 01:04:02.634
<v Guillaume Hachez>Nowadays, it's a Facebook algorithm for most of us. Is this our future? Are we destined to being subjected to this power which is being held by tech giants?

557
01:04:03.294 --> 01:04:12.174
<v Guillaume Hachez>I broadly agree in the sense that I think that an increasing amount of power will flow to those who own and control certain technologies.

558
01:04:12.894 --> 01:04:20.814
<v Guillaume Hachez>Now, that power might take a number of forms. It might take the form I was describing a moment ago, which is when people gather data about you, they're able to influence and manipulate you.

559
01:04:21.654 --> 01:04:31.414
<v Guillaume Hachez>It might take the form of perception control, where the slice of reality that we're presented of the outside world by our news feeds or our search algorithms is very, very slender.

560
01:04:31.434 --> 01:04:42.194
<v Guillaume Hachez>It's very small, and it determines our sense of right and wrong and true and false, and those who are the gatekeepers for that information, for what we know and understand of the world out there, are very powerful like that as well.

561
01:04:42.954 --> 01:04:47.974
<v Guillaume Hachez>And there's a third way as well that technology creates power, which is by writing rules that the rest of us have to follow.

562
01:04:48.714 --> 01:04:55.754
<v Guillaume Hachez>So when you try to send a tweet that is more than 280 characters, you're literally prohibited from doing so because the system won't let you.

563
01:04:56.294 --> 01:05:07.174
<v Guillaume Hachez>Likewise, your self-driving car might not drive over the speed limit if that's what its coders, if that's what its designers have chosen for it, whereas that's obviously a choice that you might have had in the past.

564
01:05:07.274 --> 01:05:15.354
<v Guillaume Hachez>So yes, I think that kind of power is being increasingly privatized. The only question remains, what do we do about it?

565
01:05:15.374 --> 01:05:21.674
<v Guillaume Hachez>And there are plenty of powerful entities in the world that are not state entities, but by and large, we regulate them.

566
01:05:22.374 --> 01:05:30.374
<v Guillaume Hachez>We require them to act in certain ways and not act in other ways that are promoting the public good. And

567
01:05:31.454 --> 01:05:41.954
<v Guillaume Hachez>we need to design and understand systems of regulation that can encourage those companies that hold great power to use it for ends that are not merely commercial ends.

568
01:05:42.554 --> 01:05:55.194
<v Guillaume Hachez>Aren't we then playing a gigantic game of cat and mouse, where a new legislation is being put in place, and then Facebook has a couple of months to think of a new idea to circumvent this new legislation,

569
01:05:56.074 --> 01:05:59.994
<v Guillaume Hachez>and at the end of the day, the power is then still being held by Facebook?

570
01:06:00.004 --> 01:06:11.734
<v Guillaume Hachez>Yes, it's the same regulatory challenge that has affected regulators in many industries for a long time, and there are other challenges, like the challenge we discussed earlier, that some companies act internationally.

571
01:06:11.794 --> 01:06:20.054
<v Guillaume Hachez>There are challenges to do with complexity and expertise, in that often the most sophisticated people in an industry work in the private sector rather than in the public sector.

572
01:06:20.644 --> 01:06:27.564
<v Guillaume Hachez>There are questions of resourcing. How do you resource sufficiently a regulator to deal properly with entities of this size?

573
01:06:27.994 --> 01:06:38.654
<v Guillaume Hachez>These are enormous challenges, and there's the question of timing. Technology, in particular technology being developed for commercial reasons, always moves faster than the laws which seeks to govern it.

574
01:06:39.894 --> 01:06:49.374
<v Guillaume Hachez>What are you gonna do? Are you gonna nationalize it? Are you gonna cancel it? The answer is, if you want to live in a free society, you let things happen, and you try to legislate and regulate as best you can.

575
01:06:49.474 --> 01:06:59.994
<v Guillaume Hachez>Mm-hmm. And I certainly know that doing nothing is not an option. Right. And there is also always a risk of doing too much, particularly by politicians who don't properly understand the problem

576
01:07:00.854 --> 01:07:04.134
<v Guillaume Hachez>lumbering in and making things worse, or indeed,

577
01:07:05.034 --> 01:07:12.414
<v Guillaume Hachez>you know, the risk of what commonly happens in the United States, which is that a nice, charming person from a large company turns up and says, "Don't worry, you don't need to understand this.

578
01:07:12.474 --> 01:07:20.230
<v Guillaume Hachez>I've written the regulations for you." And then the senator says, "Oh, yes, that's very useful. Thank you." See, for instance, the financial sector.

579
01:07:20.870 --> 01:07:28.850
<v Guillaume Hachez>So we need to avoid all of that. And I don't deny it's challenging and difficult, but I think what's useful is that we are even conceiving it in political terms.

580
01:07:29.250 --> 01:07:36.770
<v Guillaume Hachez>That's half the battle. We're gonna have to wrap up in a couple of minutes. One of my first questions to you was, Jamie, are you a pessimist?

581
01:07:36.830 --> 01:07:50.159
<v Guillaume Hachez>To which you said, "I don't think so." As a final question to you, can you come up with a series of examples of how technology actually drastically improves, or is about to drastically improve, our lives?

582
01:07:50.330 --> 01:07:57.510
<v Guillaume Hachez>Something that's specifically in terms of politics, something where the future of politics is actually bright thanks to technology.

583
01:07:57.520 --> 01:08:08.450
<v Guillaume Hachez>Well, look, I can, Guillaume, but what I would say is this: There are plenty of people out there banging the drum for technology, plenty of people saying it's going to be a great thing and transform our lives in amazing and beautiful ways.

584
01:08:09.030 --> 01:08:20.050
<v Guillaume Hachez>And already we look around us and we see our lives transformed, the way that we do commerce, the way that we do romance, the way that we do education, the way that we play games.

585
01:08:20.330 --> 01:08:31.590
<v Guillaume Hachez>These things are all being improved. And— Those are not related directly to politics. When it comes to the field, the future of politics, which is the name of your book— Yeah ...

587
01:08:34.140 --> 01:08:36.520
<v Guillaume Hachez>in which case is it actually a bright future?

588
01:08:36.670 --> 01:08:38.290
<v Guillaume Hachez>I'm gonna give you the same answer. I mean, I wish

589
01:08:38.430 --> 01:08:49.190
<v Guillaume Hachez>I could just say, "Here are all the ways that technology's improving politics," but my sole central thesis is that technology is transforming politics, and the effects are by and large not benign.

590
01:08:49.290 --> 01:08:58.210
<v Guillaume Hachez>They're not good, because we are letting politics develop according to the logic of the marketplace rather than the logic of the forum or the logic of politics itself.

591
01:08:58.510 --> 01:09:11.480
<v Guillaume Hachez>So what we need to do is get onto the playing field and start making sure that technology is engineered in a way that makes us more free, makes society more just, redistributes power, and improves democracy.

592
01:09:11.770 --> 01:09:16.560
<v Guillaume Hachez>Because I think if you just leave the systems to themselves, I do think they will corrode those. Jamie Susskind— Thanks, Guillaume ...

593
01:09:16.560 --> 01:09:31.630
<v Guillaume Hachez>thank you very much. Voilà, c'est déjà tout pour ce 27e épisode de Septante Minutes Avec. J'espère que c'était digeste. J'espère que le changement de format ne vous a pas trop dérangé.

594
01:09:32.130 --> 01:09:40.570
<v Guillaume Hachez>Comme je le disais, on revient dans deux petites semaines avec du Septante Minutes Avec pur jus, puisque je recevrai Olivia Gazalé, avec qui on va déconstruire ensemble le mythe de la virilité.

595
01:09:40.610 --> 01:09:45.170
<v Guillaume Hachez>Ça devrait être un épisode dans la lignée de ce qu'on avait fait, par exemple, avec Julie Dachez. Enfin, j'espère en tout cas.

596
01:09:45.230 --> 01:09:47.770
<v Guillaume Hachez>On verra. On verra comment ça se passe. À très vite. Salut!
